[[:db/cas 17592186521529 :question/problem "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $50\\,\\text{mph},$ on a standard test car. A sample of $25$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $174\\,\\text{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Assuming that the stopping distances are normally distributed, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu $ of these tires.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $\\,1.711\\,$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |" "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $50\\,\\text{mph},$ on a standard test car. A sample of $25$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $174\\,\\text{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Assuming that the stopping distances are normally distributed, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu $ of these tires.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $\\,1.711\\,$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186521529 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=174\\,\\text{ft},\\,n=25,\\,s=4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{24,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{24,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=25-1=24$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/52/52426e7ca1868359b5ed5c97ba1fabeaebb22b73393918d5b9031c8c6320a300.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{24,0.05}=1.711:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $1.711$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}174 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 174 & \\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=174\\,\\text{ft},\\,n=25,\\,s=4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{24,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{24,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=25-1=24$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/52/52426e7ca1868359b5ed5c97ba1fabeaebb22b73393918d5b9031c8c6320a300.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{24,0.05}=1.711:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $1.711$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}174 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 174 & \\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186521545 :question/problem "The amount of hemoglobin in the bloodstream of people living in a particular Colorado community follows a normal distribution. An analysis on a random sample of $16$ people yields a mean hemoglobin concentration of $13.1\\,\\text{g/dl}$ with a sample standard deviation of $1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean hemoglobin concentration $\\mu $ for all people in the community.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" "The amount of hemoglobin in the bloodstream of people living in a particular Colorado community follows a normal distribution. An analysis on a random sample of $16$ people yields a mean hemoglobin concentration of $13.1\\,\\text{g/dl}$ with a sample standard deviation of $1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.$ Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean hemoglobin concentration $\\mu $ for all people in the community.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186521545 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=13.1\\,\\text{g/dl},\\,n=16,\\,s=1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{15,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{15,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=16-1=15$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/03/03b3b3aec86ef22237d0013b3496993ae4cdda192540fc1c29f01ad608fcb810.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{15,0.025}=2.131:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13.1 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{1.8}{\\sqrt{16}}\\,\\text{g/dl} \\\\ 13.1 & \\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=13.1\\,\\text{g/dl},\\,n=16,\\,s=1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{15,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{15,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=16-1=15$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/03/03b3b3aec86ef22237d0013b3496993ae4cdda192540fc1c29f01ad608fcb810.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{15,0.025}=2.131:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13.1 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{1.8}{\\sqrt{16}}\\,\\text{g/dl} \\\\ 13.1 & \\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186522700 :question/problem "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who eats after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32\\%$ of all females and $5\\%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51\\%$ of the population are female. If a person who eats after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186522700 :question/worked-solution "Let $E$ be the event that a randomly selected person eats after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|E),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|E)=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E)}=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(E|M)=1-0.05=0.95,\\,P(E|{M}^{′})=1-0.32=0.68.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|E) & =\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.49)}{(0.95)(0.49)+(0.68)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈57%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." "Let $E$ be the event that a randomly selected person eats after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|E),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|E)=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E)}=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32\\%$ of all females and  $5\\%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(E|M)=1-0.05=0.95,\\,P(E|{M}^{′})=1-0.32=0.68.\n$$\n\n- Since $51\\%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|E) & =\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.49)}{(0.95)(0.49)+(0.68)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈57\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186522727 :question/problem "On a particular industrial estate, the probability of smoke on a given day is $10%$ and the probability of a dangerous fire is $1.5%.$ It is known that $90%$ of dangerous fires create smoke. What is the probability of a dangerous fire when there is smoke? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "On a particular industrial estate, the probability of smoke on a given day is $10\\%$ and the probability of a dangerous fire is $1.5\\%.$ It is known that $90\\%$ of dangerous fires create smoke. What is the probability of a dangerous fire when there is smoke? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186522727 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that there is smoke, and let $F$ be the event that there is a dangerous fire. Then, we require $P(F|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|S)=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\n- $P(S)=0.1,$ since the probability of smoke is $10%.$\n\n- $P(F)=0.015,$ since the probability of dangerous fire is $1.5%.$\n\n- $P(S|F)=0.9,$ since  $90%$ of dangerous fires make smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|S) & =\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.9\\cdot 0.015}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈0.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "Let $S$ be the event that there is smoke, and let $F$ be the event that there is a dangerous fire. Then, we require $P(F|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|S)=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\n- $P(S)=0.1,$ since the probability of smoke is $10\\%.$\n\n- $P(F)=0.015,$ since the probability of dangerous fire is $1.5\\%.$\n\n- $P(S|F)=0.9,$ since  $90\\%$ of dangerous fires make smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|S) & =\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.9\\cdot 0.015}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈0.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186522738 :question/problem "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $94%$ of those infected and $7%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $12%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected? Round your answer to four decimal places." "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $94\\%$ of those infected and $7\\%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $12\\%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected? Round your answer to four decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186522738 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $94%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $7%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.94=0.06,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.07=0.93.\n$$\n\n- Since only $12%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.12=0.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.93)(0.88)}{(0.93)(0.88)+(0.06)(0.12)} \\\\  & ≈0.9913\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places." "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $94\\%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $7\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.94=0.06,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.07=0.93.\n$$\n\n- Since only $12\\%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.12=0.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.93)(0.88)}{(0.93)(0.88)+(0.06)(0.12)} \\\\  & ≈0.9913\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186522740 :question/problem "At a soccer game, $60%$ of the spectators are men. It is known that $20%$ of the men in the crowd are over $50,$ while $30%$ of all people in the crowd are over $50.$ If a person over $50$ is picked at random from the crowd, what is the probability that the person will be a man?" "At a soccer game, $60\\%$ of the spectators are men. It is known that $20\\%$ of the men in the crowd are over $50,$ while $30\\%$ of all people in the crowd are over $50.$ If a person over $50$ is picked at random from the crowd, what is the probability that the person will be a man?"] [:db/cas 17592186522740 :question/worked-solution "Let $M$ be the event that the randomly selected person is a man, and let $F$ be the event that it is over $50.$ Then, we require $P(M|F),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|F)=\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|M)=0.2,$ since $20%$ of the men at the game are over $50.$\n\n- $P(M)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of all the spectators are men.\n\n- $P(F)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of all spectators are over $50.$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|F) & =\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $M$ be the event that the randomly selected person is a man, and let $F$ be the event that it is over $50.$ Then, we require $P(M|F),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|F)=\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|M)=0.2,$ since $20\\%$ of the men at the game are over $50.$\n\n- $P(M)=0.6,$ since $60\\%$ of all the spectators are men.\n\n- $P(F)=0.3,$ since $30\\%$ of all spectators are over $50.$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|F) & =\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186522742 :question/problem "On an MLB team, $75%$ of all players are American citizens. It is known that $15%$ of them also speak Spanish, while $25%$ of all players on the team speak Spanish. If a player who speaks Spanish is randomly selected for a TV interview, what is the probability that he is American? Express your answer as a percentage." "On an MLB team, $75\\%$ of all players are American citizens. It is known that $15\\%$ of them also speak Spanish, while $25\\%$ of all players on the team speak Spanish. If a player 