 answer to $2$ decimal places." "A laboratory test for a particular allergy is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $96\\%$ of allergy sufferers and $3\\%$ of non-allergic patients tested positive for the allergy. Only $4\\%$ of the entire population has the allergy. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is an allergy sufferer? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186522778 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy testing positive is $96%,$ and the probability of a non-allergic person testing positive is $3%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.96,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since only $4%$ of the population suffers the allergy, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.04,\\,P({A}^{′})=1-0.04=0.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|T) & =\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.96)(0.04)}{(0.96)(0.04)+(0.03)(0.96)} \\\\  & ≈0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy testing positive is $96\\%,$ and the probability of a non-allergic person testing positive is $3\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.96,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since only $4\\%$ of the populatio