viders.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" "A poll of $800$ randomly selected U.S. Internet subscribers reveals that only $560$ of those interviewed are satisfied with their current internet provider. Find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. internet users that are satisfied with their current internet providers.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186523949 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. internet users,\n\n- some internet subscribers are satisfied with their current internet provider, and some aren't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=800,$ and\n\n- the proportion of Internet subscribers from the sample who are satisfied with their current internet provider is found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{560}{800}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=800\\cdot 0.7=560>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=800\\cdot (1-0.7)=240>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{800}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. internet users,\n\n- some internet subscribers are satisfied with their current internet provider, and some aren't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=800,$ and\n\n- the proportion of Internet subscribers from the sample who are satisfied with their current internet provider is found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{560}{800}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=800\\cdot 0.7=560>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=800\\cdot (1-0.7)=240>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{800}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186523954 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=160$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $5%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" "Consider a sample of size $n=160$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $5\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [:db/cas 17592186523954 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=160$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=5%=0.05,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=160\\cdot 0.05=8>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=160\\cdot (1-0.05)=152