}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.05\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{160}} \\\\  & 0.05\\pm 0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186523969 :question/problem "Olivia and Harper are musicians. In the previous season, the decimal proportion of Olivia’s performances that were solos was $0.55,$ while $40%$ of Harper’s performances were solos. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\nII. More than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\nIII. Olivia had a greater percentage of solo performances compared to Harper." "Olivia and Harper are musicians. In the previous season, the decimal proportion of Olivia’s performances that were solos was $0.55,$ while $40\\%$ of Harper’s performances were solos. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\nII. More than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\nIII. Olivia had a greater percentage of solo performances compared to Harper."] [:db/cas 17592186523969 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.55>0.5,\n$$\n\nmore than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n40%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $0.55$ and $40%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.55\\times 100=55%\n$$\n\nSince $55%>40%,$ Olivia had a **greater** percentage of solo performances compared to Harper.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.55>0.5,\n$$\n\nmore than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n40\\%<50\\%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $0.55$ and $40\\%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.55\\times 100=55\\%\n$$\n\nSince $55\\%>40\\%,$ Olivia had a **greater** percentage of solo performances compared to Harper.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186523987 :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=3{csch}^{2}⁡x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=3{csch}^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186524008 :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186524034 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=75$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=75$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186524034 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=75$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=75\\cdot 0.4=30>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=75\\cdot (1-0.4)=45>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{75}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.146\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $n=75$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=75\\cdot 0.4=30>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=75\\cdot (1-0.4)=45>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{75}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.146\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186524963 :question/problem "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $56%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $96%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $14%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $80%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $30%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3,$ and $90%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\nIf $45%$ of all customers are teenagers and $92%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, what is the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $56\\%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $96\\%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $14\\%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $80\\%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $30\\%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3,$ and $90\\%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\nIf $45\\%$ of all customers are teenagers and $92\\%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, what is the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186524963 :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.56 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.96 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.14 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.8 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(S|{P}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.56+0.14+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $92%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.92.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager, which is represented as $P(T|S).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.92)(0.45)}{(0.96)(0.56)+(0.8)(0.14)+(0.9)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.414}{0.9196} \\\\  & ≈0.450,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.56 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.96 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.14 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.8 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(S|{P}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.56+0.14+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45\\%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $92\\%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.92.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager, which is represented as $P(T|S).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.92)(0.45)}{(0.96)(0.56)+(0.8)(0.14)+(0.9)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.414}{0.9196} \\\\  & ≈0.450,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186524974 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186524976 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(x)}{\\sqrt{x}},$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}($ \n{{field-1}} $-\\frac{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{2x})$" "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(x)}{\\sqrt{x}},$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}($ \n{{field-1}} $-\\frac{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{2x})$"] [:db/cas 17592186524986 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x}{x}\\,-$ \n{{field-1}}" "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x}{x}\\,-$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186524988 :question/problem "$$\