To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of emergencies that firefighters attended in the first year, which is $40$ emergencies. The percentage is $165%,$ and the part is the total number of emergencies firefighters attended in the second year.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n165%=165÷100=1.65\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165%\\times 40 \\\\  & =1.65\\times 40 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n165%=\\frac{165}{100}=\\frac{165÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{33}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{20}\\times 40 \\\\  & =33\\times \\frac{40}{20} \\\\  & =33\\times 2 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year." "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of emergencies that firefighters attended in the first year, which is $40$ emergencies. The percentage is $165\\%,$ and the part is the total number of emergencies firefighters attended in the second year.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n165\\%=165÷100=1.65\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165\\%\\times 40 \\\\  & =1.65\\times 40 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165\\%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n165\\%=\\frac{165}{100}=\\frac{165÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{33}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165\\%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{20}\\times 40 \\\\  & =33\\times \\frac{40}{20} \\\\  & =33\\times 2 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165\\%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year."] [:db/cas 17592186525015 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+g(x).$ What is $g(x)?$" "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+g(x).$ What is $g(x)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186525019 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y={e}^{-x}-\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡{e}^{-x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y={e}^{-x}-\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡{e}^{-x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186525022 :question/problem "The return on a risky investment can be negative (meaning that the investor loses money) or positive. Assuming that the return on a particular investment is uniformly distributed between $-7.5%$ and $12.5%$, what is the mean return of this investment?" "The return on a risky investment can be negative (meaning that the investor loses money) or positive. Assuming that the return on a particular investment is uniformly distributed between $-7.5\\%$ and $12.5\\%$, what is the mean return of this investment?"] [:db/cas 17592186525022 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the return on the investment. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-7.5,12.5],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-7.5,12.5],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-7.5+12.5}{2}=2.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean return on the investment is $2.5%.$" "Let $X$ represent the return on the investment. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-7.5,12.5],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-7.5,12.5],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-7.5+12.5}{2}=2.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean return on the investment is $2.5\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186525024 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\int (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+1)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\int (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+1)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525026 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=6\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(3x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y=6\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(3x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186525033 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=2\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(5x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $y=2\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(5x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186525033 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(2\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(5x)\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}})\\cdot (5x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5x\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(2\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(5x)\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}})\\cdot (5x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5x\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525036 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-3),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y=-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-3),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186525039 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=2\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(-x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $y=2\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(-x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186525041 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{x}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x>0.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $y=-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{x}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x>0.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186525047 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{{e}^{6}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\int \\frac{{e}^{6}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525053 :question/problem "Apples produced by a particular farm are of three varieties: $1,2,$ and $3.$\n\n- $30%$ of the apples are of variety $1,$ and $45%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $20%$ of the apples are of variety $2,$ and $15%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $50%$ of the apples are of variety $3,$ and $10%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\nIf $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, and $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weighs more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "Apples produced by a particular farm are of three varieties: $1,2,$ and $3.$\n\n- $30\\%$ of the apples are of variety $1,$ and $45\\%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $20\\%$ of the apples are of variety $2,$ and $15\\%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $50\\%$ of the apples are of variety $3,$ and $10\\%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\nIf $75\\%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, and $20\\%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weighs more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186525053 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that an apple is sold to the supermarket, $W$ be the event that the apple weights more than $200\\,g,$ and ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},{V}_{2}$ be the events that an apple is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.45 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(W|{V}_{3})=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${V}_{1}⊔{V}_{2}⊔{V}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({V}_{1})+P({V}_{2})+P({V}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=0.75.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weigh more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket, which is represented as $P(S|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|W) & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.75)}{(0.3)(0.45)+(0.2)(0.15)+(0.5)(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.215} \\\\  & ≈0.698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $S$ be the event that an apple is sold to the supermarket, $W$ be the event that the apple weights more than $200\\,g,$ and ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},{V}_{2}$ be the events that an apple is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.45 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(W|{V}_{3})=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${V}_{1}⊔{V}_{2}⊔{V}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({V}_{1})+P({V}_{2})+P({V}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $75\\%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=0.75.\n$$\n\n- Since $20\\%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weigh more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket, which is represented as $P(S|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|W) & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.75)}{(0.3)(0.45)+(0.2)(0.15)+(0.5)(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.215} \\\\  & ≈0.698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186525057 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{2}+1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{2}+1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186525059 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arsech}⁡({3}^{x}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $y=\\operatorname{arsech}⁡({3}^{x}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186525062 :question/problem "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1,$ team $2,$ and team $3.$\n\n- $54%$ of the children are in team $1,$ and $20%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $32%$ of the children are in team $2,$ and $30%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $14%$ of the children are in team $3,$ and $15%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\nIf $10%$ of the children can play in all positions, and $94%$ of children that can play in all positions have been playing for over a year, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1,$ team $2,$ and team $3.$\n\n- $54\\%$ of the children are in team $1,$ and $20\\%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $32\\%$ of the children are in team $2,$ and $30\\%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $14\\%$ of the children are in team $3,$ and $15\\%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\nIf $10\\%$ of the children can play in all positions, and $94\\%$ of children that can play in all positions have been playing for over a year, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186525062 :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in all positions, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over a year, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.54 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.14 &  & P(I|{T}_{3})=0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.54+0.32+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children can play in all positions, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $94%$ of children who play in all positions have been playing for over a year, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.94.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions, which is represented as $P(B|I).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.94)(0.1)}{(0.2)(0.54)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.15)(0.14)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.094}{0.225} \\\\  & ≈0.418,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in all positions, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over a year, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.54 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.14 &  & P(I|{T}_{3})=0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.54+0.32+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10\\%$ of children can play in all positions, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $94\\%$ of children who play in all positions have been playing for over a year, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.94.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions, which is represented as $P(B|I).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.94)(0.1)}{(0.2)(0.54)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.15)(0.14)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.094}{0.225} \\\\  & ≈0.418,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186525063 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=-4\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(3-2x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{|3-2x|}$\n{{field-1}}" "If $y=-4\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(3-2x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{|3-2x|}$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186525985 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{30} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct o