es by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=5,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533995 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"] [:db/cas 17592186534011 :question/problem "There were approximately $28,\\,000$ factory jobs in the city in $2020.$  Economic forecasts predict that this number will grow by $35%$ by the year $2030.$  Which of the following is closest to the projected number of factory jobs in $2030?$" "There were approximately $28,\\,000$ factory jobs in the city in $2020.$  Economic forecasts predict that this number will grow by $35\\%$ by the year $2030.$  Which of the following is closest to the projected number of factory jobs in $2030?$"] [:db/cas 17592186534011 :question/worked-solution "To find the projected number of jobs in $2030,$ we apply a $35%$ increase to the $2020$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $35%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+35%=135%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.35:$\n\n$$\n28,\\,000\\times 1.35=37,\\,800\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of factory jobs in $2030$ is $37,\\,800.$" "To find the projected number of jobs in $2030,$ we apply a $35\\%$ increase to the $2020$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+35\\%=135\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nFi