an write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $30%$ of the students rode a roller coaster." "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of students who rode a roller coaster, which is $150,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of students, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=150÷500=\\frac{150}{500}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{500}=\\frac{150÷5}{500÷5}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $30\\%$ of the students rode a roller coaster."] [:db/cas 17592186540022 :question/problem "![](images/62/6240abf268497f82475003619689d979b0b00678b4d49972ff2fbcf1fa866a9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a cubic gas tank. One-fifth of the volume of the tank is filled with gas. If the fuel costs $$0.0016$ per cubic centimeter, how much would it cost to fill the tank to capacity?" "![](images/62/6240abf268497f82475003619689d979b0b00678b4d49972ff2fbcf1fa866a9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a cubic gas tank. One-fifth of the volume of the tank is filled with gas. If the fuel costs $\\$0.0016$ per cubic centimeter, how much would it cost to fill the tank to capacity?"] [:db/cas 17592186540024 :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=30$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=30$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186540024 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540029 :question/problem "John wonders what bottle of juice to buy from three different types available at the convenience store:\n\n- Bottle $A$ contains $20$ ounces, and costs $$3.00\\,.$\n\n- Bottle $B$ contains $32$ ounces, and costs $$4.80\\,.$\n\n- Bottle $C$ contains $10$ ounces, and costs $$1.80\\,.$\n\nWhich bottle of juice is the best deal?" "John wonders what bottle of juice to buy from three different types available at the convenience store:\n\n- Bottle $A$ contains $20$ ounces, and costs $\\$3.00\\,.$\n\n- Bottle $B$ contains $32$ ounces, and costs $\\$4.80\\,.$\n\n- Bottle $C$ contains $10$ ounces, and costs $\\$1.80\\,.$\n\nWhich bottle of juice is the best deal?"] [:db/cas 17592186540029 :question/worked-solution "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bottles. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $A$,  we get\n\n$$\n3.00÷20=0.15\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $A$ is $$0.15$  per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $B$,  we get\n\n$$\n4.80÷32=0.15\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $B$ is $$0.15$  per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $C$,  we get\n\n$$\n1.80÷10=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $C$ is $$0.18$  per ounce.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bottles $A$ and $B$ have the lowest unit price. Therefore, bottles $A$ and $B$ are the best deal." "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bottles. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $A$,  we get\n\n$$\n3.00÷20=0.15\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $A$ is $\\$0.15$  per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $B$,  we get\n\n$$\n4.80÷32=0.15\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $B$ is $\\$0.15$  per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bottle $C$,  we get\n\n$$\n1.80÷10=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bottle $C$ is $\\$0.18$  per ounce.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bottles $A$ and $B$ have the lowest unit price. Therefore, bottles $A$ and $B$ are the best deal."] [:db/cas 17592186540040 :question/problem "Anne needs to buy copper wire, the unit price for the copper wire is $$0.08$ per foot. How many feet of cooper wire can Anne buy with $$1?$" "Anne needs to buy copper wire, the unit price for the copper wire is $\\$0.08$ per foot. How many feet of cooper wire can Anne buy with $\\$1?$"] [:db/cas 17592186540040 :question/worked-solution "We are given the unit rate of $$0.08$ per foot (dollars per foot). To find the amount of copper wire that Anne can buy for $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in feet per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n0.08\\,\\text{dollars per foot}\\,\\to \\,1÷0.08=12.5\\,\\text{feet per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $12.5$ feet per dollar. Therefore, Anne can buy $12.5$ feet of copper wire." "We are given the unit rate of $\\$0.08$ per foot (dollars per foot). To find the amount of copper wire that Anne can buy for $\\$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in feet per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n0.08\\,\\text{dollars per foot}\\,\\to \\,1÷0.08=12.5\\,\\text{feet per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $12.5$ feet per dollar. Therefore, Anne can buy $12.5$ feet of copper wire."] [:db/cas 17592186540041 :question/problem "Louis visits a snack shop, the unit price for nuts is $$8$ per pound. How many pounds of nuts can Louis buy for $$1?$" "Louis visits a snack shop, the unit price for 