t the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-11=8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{32}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈3.118\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 8\\pm 3.118\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540104 :question/problem "In a town, blood tests are being carried out on all inhabitants to determine whether they have caught a virus. On average, $30%$ of the inhabitants test positive for the virus. What is the probability that the first positive test result will be made on the $3$rd test or later?" "In a town, blood tests are being carried out on all inhabitants to determine whether they have caught a virus. On average, $30\\%$ of the inhabitants test positive for the virus. What is the probability that the first positive test result will be made on the $3$rd test or later?"] [:db/cas 17592186540104 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found. Since the chance of a single patient being infected is $30%,$ the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7](0.3) \\\\  & =0.51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first positive patient on the $3$rd test or later is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.51 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found. Since the chance of a single patient being infected is $30\\%,$ the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30\\%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7](0.3) \\\\  & =0.51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first positive patient on the $3$rd test or later is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.51 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540111 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=58$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=86$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=34$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=43,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=58$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=86$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=34$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=43,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [:db/cas 17592186540111 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542120 :question/problem "A backpack costs $$64$ before tax. If a $7.75%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" "A backpack costs $\\$64$ before tax. If a $7.75\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?"] [:db/cas 17592186542120 :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $7.75%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.75%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+7.75%=107.75%=1.0775\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0775:$\n\n$$\n64\\times 1.0775=68.96.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the backpack after tax is $$68.96.$" "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $7.75\\%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.75\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+7.75\\%=107.75\\%=1.0775\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0775:$\n\n$$\n64\\times 1.0775=68.96.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the backpack after tax is $\\$68.96.$"] [:db/cas 17592186544865 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of students that failed the exam, which is $12,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of students, which is $40.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=12÷40=\\frac{12}{40}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{40}=\\frac{12÷4}{40÷4}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{10}=\\fra