ign=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186545824 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the amount of water, which is $1\\,400$ liters, and\n\n- the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $2\\,000$ liters.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=1\\,400÷2\\,000=\\frac{14}{20}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{20}=\\frac{14\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{70}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{70}{100}=70%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70%$ of the tank is filled with water." "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the amount of water, which is $1\\,400$ liters, and\n\n- the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $2\\,000$ liters.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=1\\,400÷2\\,000=\\frac{14}{20}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{20}=\\frac{14\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{70}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\