h=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186545840 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186545867 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}x\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}x\\sqrt{2(1-{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}x\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}x\\sqrt{2(1-{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186545875 :question/problem "$$\n\nCalculate $\\int \\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$" "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186545875 :question/worked-solution "$$\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186546963 :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a student correctly solves $4$ questions in a $5$-question math test if the student has a $75%$ probability of correctly answering each question.\n\nII. The probability that $42$ flights out of $120$ arrive early at an airport if the probability of a single flight arriving early is $0.45$ for each flight.\n\nIII. The probability of getting $4$ ones on $10$ rolls of a $4$-sided die." "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a student correctly solves $4$ questions in a $5$-question math test if the student has a $75\\%$ probability of correctly answering each question.\n\nII. The probability that $42$ flights out of $120$ arrive early at an airport if the probability of a single flight arriving early is $0.45$ for each flight.\n\nIII. The probability of getting $4$ ones on $10$ rolls of a $4$-sided die."] [:db/cas 17592186547041 :question/problem "An ergonomist investigates $4$ factories. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the money spent by factory owners on safety training courses, and $y,$ the number of accidents per year in the factories. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Money spent (hundreds of $$$)** | **$10$** | **$15$** | **$12$** | **$23$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of accidents** | $3$ | $6$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=15$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.45$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nHint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation." "An ergonomist investigates $4$ factories. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the money spent by factory owners on safety training courses, and $y,$ the number of accidents per year in the factories. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Money spent (hundreds of \\$)** | **$10$** | **$15$** | **$12$** | **$23$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of accidents** | $3$ | $6$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=15$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.45$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nHint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation."] [:db/cas 17592186547055 :question/problem "Museum attendance increased to $120%$ of last year’s total. Of those visitors, $90%$ also attended the art exhibit. What percentage of last year’s total attended the art exhibit?" "Museum attendance increased to $120\\%$ of last year’s total. Of those visitors, $90\\%$ also attended the art exhibit. What percentage of last year’s total attended the art exhibit?"] [:db/cas 17592186547055 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the number of visitors last year.\n\nWe're told that attendance increased to $120%$ of last year’s total. Thus, the new number of visitors is $1.2x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those visitors, $90%$ attended the art exhibit. So, the number of visitors who attended the art exhibit is\n\n$$\n0.9(1.2x)=1.08x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $1.08x$ represents $108%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $108%$ of last year’s total attended the art exhibit." "Let $x$ be the number of visitors last year.\n\nWe're told that attendance increased to $120\\%$ of last year’s total. Thus, the new number of visitors is $1.2x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those visitors, $90\\%$ attended the art exhibit. So, the number of visitors who attended the art exhibit is\n\n$$\n0.9(1.2x)=1.08x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $1.08x$ represents $108\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $108\\%$ of last year’s total attended the art exhibit."] [:db/cas 17592186547059 :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $60$ times. The archer has a $44%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $30$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |" "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $60$ times. The archer has a $44\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $30$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186548034 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(0.2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=0.2$ is $0.2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0.2-0}{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 0.1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+0.1=0.1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0.1\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0.2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0.2\\cdot 0.1=0.02 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0.1+0.1=0.2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0.02=0.02\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.02$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.02$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $f(0.2)≈0.02.$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(0.2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=0.2$ is $0.2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0.2-0}{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 0.1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+0.1=0.1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0.1\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0.2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0.2\\cdot 0.1=0.02 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0.1+0.1=0.2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0.02=0.02\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.02$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.02$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $f(0.2)≈0.02.$"] [:db/cas 17592186548044 :question/problem "In a factory, the average amount of sugar in a cookie is $15$ grams. However, $10%$ of cookies contain no more than $14$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the amount of sugar in a cookie, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" "In a factory, the average amount of sugar in a cookie is $15$ grams. However, $10\\%$ of cookies contain no more than $14$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the amount of sugar in a cookie, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186548976 :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186548976 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{csch}^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{csch}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡u){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{9}^{2}+1}{{9}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{4}^{2}+1}{{4}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{41}{40}+\\frac{17}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{240}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{csch}^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{csch}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡u){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{9}^{2}+1}{{9}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{4}^{2}+1}{{4}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{41}{40}+\\frac{17}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{240}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549010 :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/17/172826a7fa27ac8957f120e15622cf0c96b0e8df66e5f6fd3d7d99ab0b32c5f0.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $20\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<20\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 800-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{800}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =400 \\\\ l & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,20)$ | $20$ | $(20,20\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $25$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(20) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot \\sqrt{800-{(20)}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{800-400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/17/172826a7fa27ac8957f120e15622cf0c96b0e8df66e5f6fd3d7d99ab0b32c5f0.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $20\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<20\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 800-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{800}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =400 \\\\ l & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,20)$ | $20$ | $(20,20\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $25$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(20) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot \\sqrt{800-{(20)}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{800-400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549013 :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186549019 :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $16$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<16.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(l)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 256-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =256 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =128 \\\\ l & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ | $8\\sqrt{2}$ | $(8\\sqrt{2},16)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $12$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(8\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{256-(8\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $16$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<16.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(l)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 256-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =256 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =128 \\\\ l & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ | $8\\sqrt{2}$ | $(8\\sqrt{2},16)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $12$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(8\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{256-(8\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549020 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,