d align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186549034 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549040 :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/29/296e866a3104f0d2a7891726274578bcdffb84853ce896e8723e2b742d22ac27.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10\\sqrt{2}+l+\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10\\sqrt{2},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =200-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =200 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ l & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,10)$ | $10$ | $(10,10\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $11$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+10 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+20 \\\\  & =10(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/29/296e866a3104f0d2a7891726274578bcdffb84853ce896e8723e2b742d22ac27.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10\\sqrt{2}+l+\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10\\sqrt{2},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =200-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =200 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ l & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,10)$ | $10$ | $(10,10\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $11$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+10 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+20 \\\\  & =10(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549045 :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}3\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}3\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186549045 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}3\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(u)\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(u){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)+\\operatorname{sech}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8+{8}^{-1}}{2})}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{65}{16})}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{65}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}3\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(u)\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(u){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)+\\operatorname{sech}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8+{8}^{-1}}{2})}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{65}{16})}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{65}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549057 :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =4+l+\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $4,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<4.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =16-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =8 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $3$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\sqrt{2}) & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-8} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =4+l+\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $4,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<4.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =16-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =8 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\sqrt{2}) & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-8} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186549926 :question/problem "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $3.3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, and $2%$ of female children have scoliosis, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25\\%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $3\\%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30\\%$ of children in the sample practice one sport, and $2.5\\%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $45\\%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $3.3\\%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $52\\%$ of children in the entire sample are female, and $2\\%$ of female children have scoliosis, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186549926 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52\\%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2\\%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186549938 :question/problem "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $2.8%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $48%$ of children in the entire sample are female, $2.6%$ of female children have scoliosis, and $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25\\%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $2.5\\%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30\\%$ of children in the sample practice one sport.\n\n- $45\\%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $2.8\\%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $48\\%$ of children in the entire sample are female, $2.6\\%$ of female children have scoliosis, and $46\\%$ of children with scoliosis are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186549938 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.48.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.6%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C∣F)=0.026.\n$$\n\n- Since $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F∣C)=0.46.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis, which is represented as $P(C∣{S}_{1}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C∣{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C∣{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C∣{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{(0.026)(0.48)}{(0.25)(0.025)+P(C∣{S}_{1})(0.3)+(0.028)(0.45)} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C∣{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.46(0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})) & =0.0125 \\\\ 0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46} \\\\ 0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189 \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.3}(\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189) \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & ≈0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48\\%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=