(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=60$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=60\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=60\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$" "The number of downloads $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=60$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=60\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=60\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551304 :question/problem "A cattle rancher has three ranches, where $34%$ of the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ on ranch $C.$ If $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, and the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is a dairy cow is $37%,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow?" "A cattle rancher has three ranches, where $34\\%$ of the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36\\%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30\\%$ on ranch $C.$ If $25\\%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20\\%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, and the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is a dairy cow is $37\\%,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow?"] [:db/cas 17592186551304 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a selected cow is a dairy cow, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected cow is a dairy cow, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since $34%$ of all the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ are on ranch $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.34,\\,P(B)=0.36,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.25,\\,P(D|B)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is  a dairy cow is $37%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.37.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.37 & =(0.25)(0.34)+(0.2)(0.36)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.37 & =0.085+0.072+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.213 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow is $71%.$" "Let $D$ be the event that a selected cow is a dairy cow, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected cow is a dairy cow, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since $34\\%$ of all the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36\\%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30\\%$ are on ranch $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.34,\\,P(B)=0.36,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25\\%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20\\%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.25,\\,P(D|B)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is  a dairy cow is $37\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.37.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.37 & =(0.25)(0.34)+(0.2)(0.36)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.37 & =0.085+0.072+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.213 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow is $71\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552426 :question/problem "A call center manager knows that $40%$ of the calls are complaints. If the manager randomly samples $80$ calls, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ of them are complaints. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "A call center manager knows that $40\\%$ of the calls are complaints. If the manager randomly samples $80$ calls, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ of them are complaints. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186552449 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population where $30%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $40%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population where $30\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $40\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186552503 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ students from a class, where $60%$ of the students in the school have an IQ greater than $105.$\n\nII. A sample of $60$ paid TV customers, where $80%$ of the customers watch TV more than $8$ hours per week.\n\nIII. A sample of $40$ players in a team, $90%$ of the players in the league are right-handed.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ students from a class, where $60\\%$ of the students in the school have an IQ greater than $105.$\n\nII. A sample of $60$ paid TV customers, where $80\\%$ of the customers watch TV more than $8$ hours per week.\n\nIII. A sample of $40$ players in a team, $90\\%$ of the players in the league are right-handed.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186552503 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.6) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60(0.8) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.8) \\\\  & =12 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.6) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60(0.8) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.8) \\\\  & =12 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186553492 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186553521 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x+1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "If $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x+1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186553527 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 