\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(20-16)+(1-2)(18-16) \\\\  & +(3-2)(15-16)+(4-2)(11-16) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-2-1-10 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{21}{10}=-2.1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =16-(-2.1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2.1x+20.2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=8$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ 8 & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ -12.2 & =-2.1x \\\\ x & ≈6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes $6$ years for the motorcycle to have a value of $$8\\,000.$\n\nNote: Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=8$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(20-16)+(1-2)(18-16) \\\\  & +(3-2)(15-16)+(4-2)(11-16) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-2-1-10 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{21}{10}=-2.1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =16-(-2.1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2.1x+20.2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=8$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ 8 & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ -12.2 & =-2.1x \\\\ x & ≈6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes $6$ years for the motorcycle to have a value of $\\$8\\,000.$\n\nNote: Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=8$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable."] [:db/cas 17592186553566 :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=(x+2)\\,\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,+$ \n{{field-1}}" "If $f(x)=(x+2)\\,\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,+$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186553571 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{20{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x-8}{2{x}^{2}-4x+3}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{2{x}^{2}-4x+3},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $20{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x-8$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ | __ |  |  | $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ |" "Express $\\frac{20{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x-8}{2{x}^{2}-4x+3}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{2{x}^{2}-4x+3},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.\n\n| $20{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x-8$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ | __ |  |  | $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186554542 :question/problem "| **Number of hours ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grade ($y$)** | $75$ | $82$ | $90$ | $95$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours four students spent studying for a math exam and the grades they obtained. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.9,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" "| **Number of hours ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grade ($y$)** | $75$ | $82$ | $90$ | $95$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours four students spent studying for a math exam and the grades they obtained. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.9,$ find a $98\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186554542 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=61.7+6.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=61.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-3.5)^{2}+(3-3.5)^{2}+(4-3.5)^{2}+(5-3.5)^{2} \\\\  & =2.25+0.25+0.25+2.25 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{0.9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{5})}≈10.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=61.7\\pm 10.9\n$$" "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=61.7+6.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i