 value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=5+0.4x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{4.3}^{2}}{330})}≈2.5\n