\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$" "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186554639 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{9},{y}_{9})$ of $n=9$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.16,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=240,\n$$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.895)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $7$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{9},{y}_{9})$ of $n=9$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.16,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=240,\n$$ find a $90\\%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.895)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $7$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186554639 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=3.4-0.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.895)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{7,0.05}=1.895.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.895\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1.16}{240}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.6\\pm 0.1\n$$" "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=3.4-0.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.895)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{7,0.05}=1.895.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.895\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1.16}{240}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.6\\pm 0.1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186554645 :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,12).$ So, our actual value is $y=12.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $10.$ So, our estimated value is $\\widehat{y}=10.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab5e194ac03482b1cee6bdb7aa35e323b40a1ad63769e60acbb000adb7aa1e82.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $$2\\,000.$" "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,12).$ So, our actual value is $y=12.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $10.$ So, our estimated value is $\\widehat{y}=10.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab5e194ac03482b1cee6bdb7aa35e323b40a1ad63769e60acbb000adb7aa1e82.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $\\$2\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186555768 :question/problem "Mike purchased an $80$'s baseball card for $$50.$ If the value of the card increases by $8%$ each year, what expression gives the card's value after $10$ years?" "Mike purchased an $80$'s baseball card for $\\$50.$ If the value of the card increases by $8\\%$ each year, what expression gives the card's value after $10$ years?"] [:db/cas 17592186555768 :question/worked-solution "The card's value is $$50$, and this number increases by $8%$ each year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $10$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}$ dollars." "The card's value is $\\$50$, and this number increases by $8\\%$ each year.\n\n$8\\%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $10$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}$ dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186555799 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186555799 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60%=0.6,\\,n=200.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(200,0.6).\n$$" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60\\%=0.6,\\,n=200.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(200,0.6).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555810 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186555810 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=72%=0.72,\\,n=80.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(80,0.72).\n$$" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=72\\%=0.72,\\,n=80.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(80,0.72).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555835 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186555835 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=5%=0.05,\\,n=120.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(120,0.05)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05\\cdot (1-0.05) \\\\  & =5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=5\\%=0.05,\\,n=120.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(120,0.05)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05\\cdot (1-0.05) \\\\  & =5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555845 :question/problem "Suppose that $38%$ of a city's adult population like romance movies. A random sample of $n=30$ adults is taken from the population and asked about their favorite movie genre.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of people in the sample who like romance movies? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose that $38\\%$ of a city's adult population like romance movies. A random sample of $n=30$ adults is taken from the population and asked about their favorite movie genre.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of people in the sample who like romance movies? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186555868 :question/problem "Rose's initial salary was $$10\\,000$, and her salary increased by $12%$ per year. To the nearest $$10$, what was Rose's salary $3$ years later?" "Rose's initial salary was $\\$10\\,000$, and her salary increased by $12\\%$ per year. To the nearest $\\$10$, what was Rose's salary $3$ years later?"] [:db/cas 17592186555868 :question/worked-solution "Rose received $$10\\,000$ initially, and this amount increased by $12%$ each year.\n\n$12%$ as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, an increase of $12%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.12=1.12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money received after $3$ years, we get\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}≈14\\,050.\n$$\n\nAfter $3$ years, Rose received approximately $14\\,050$ dollars." "Rose received $\\$10\\,000$ initially, and this amount increased by $12\\%$ each year.\n\n$12\\%$ as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, an increase of $12\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.12=1.12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money received after $3$ years, we get\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}≈14\\,050.\n$$\n\nAfter $3$ years, Rose received approximately $14\\,050$ dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186558483 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=1\\,000$ represents the initial population of ants\n\n- $b=1.5$ is the growth factor\n\n- $t=4$ is the time in months since the start of the observations\n\nFor the factor $(1.5)^{4},$ the growth rate $(1.5)$ means that the population grows by $50%$ per month, and the exponent $(4)$ gives the number of months. \n\nTherefore, the ant population grew by a factor of $(1.5)^{4}$ over the first $4$ months." "The given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=1\\,000$ represents the initial population of ants\n\n- $b=1.5$ is the growth factor\n\n- $t=4$ is the time in months since the start of the observations\n\nFor the factor $(1.5)^{4},$ the growth rate $(1.5)$ means that the population grows by $50\\%$ per month, and the exponent $(4)$ gives the number of months. \n\nTherefore, the ant population grew by a factor of $(1.5)^{4}$ over the first $4$ months."] [:db/cas 17592186558494 :question/problem "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{y}+8}{{y}^{3/2}}\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |" "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{y}+8}{{y}^{3/2}}\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186558505 :question/problem "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=(2y+1)^{2}-6\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |" "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=(2y+1)^{2}-6\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186558510 :question/problem "Austin's average monthly profit has been decreasing at a rate of $6%$ every year. If he initially recorded an average monthly profit of $$58\\,000$, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ Austin's average monthly profit since the start of the decline?" "Austin's average monthly profit has been decreasing at a rate of $6\\%$ every year. If he initially recorded an average monthly profit of $\\$58\\,000$, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ Austin's average monthly profit since the start of the decline?"] [:db/cas 17592186558510 :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=58\\,000$ is the initial average monthly profit.\n\n- $r=0.06$ is the decay rate ($6%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (1-0.06)^{t}=58\\,000\\cdot (0.94)^{t}.\n$$" "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=58\\,000$ is the initial average monthly profit.\n\n- $r=0.06$ is the decay rate ($6\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (1-0.06)^{t}=58\\,000\\cdot (0.94)^{t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558525 :question/problem "An adult drinks a cup of coffee that contains $110$ mg of caffeine, and each hour the amount of caffeine in their body decreases by $15%.$ To the nearest integer, how much caffeine will be left in their system after $11$ hours?" "An adult drinks a cup of coffee that contains $110$ mg of caffeine, and each hour the amount of caffeine in their body decreases by $15\\%.$ To the nearest integer, how much caffeine will be left in their system after $11$ hours?"]]
